.TH BIDET 1 "October 2026" "BIDeT 4D" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bidet \- draw text or terminal art as a picture, in a style: flat, 3D, or animated
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bidet
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-P\fR \fIpreset\fR ...] [\fITEXT\fR | \fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bidet
is the simple front end of the BIDeT 4D tools
.BR bifin (1),
.BR bifop (1)
and
.BR bifout (1).
Give it some words, a file of terminal art, a terminal session (a
.I .vt
file) or a BIF, and it draws a picture: on a terminal, in whatever it can show, or into a PNG, GIF, SIXEL, ANSI or Tektronix file named with
.BR \-o .
It is sugar over the pipeline, not a second implementation:
.B bidet \-n
prints the
.B bifin | bifop | bifout
command line it would run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-P " \fIname\fR, " \-\-preset " \fIname"
A preset (a named style); repeat to combine, the later one winning:
.B \-P arc \-P popout \-P chrome
is a chrome arch that pops out of the screen.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list\-presets
List the presets and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR, " \-\-output " FILE"
Write the picture here (.png, .gif, .six, .ans, .tek, .bif); \fB\-\fR is standard output.  Without it the picture goes to the
terminal if standard output is one, else it is a PNG.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
The format, if the file name or the terminal should not decide: \fBpng\fR, \fBgif\fR, \fBsixel\fR, \fBkitty\fR, \fBiterm\fR,
\fBansi\fR (coloured text), \fBtek\fR (Tektronix strokes), \fBtek\-hatch\fR, \fBtek\-dots\fR, \fBtek\-contour\fR, \fBbif\fR, or
\fBauto\fR, which on a terminal is the default: SIXEL if the terminal has it, kitty or iTerm2 if that is what it is, else coloured text.
See
.BR bifout (1).
.TP
.BI \-w " PX" "\fR, " \-\-width " PX"
Draw this many pixels wide (default: the terminal's width, or, for a file, 1000 for text, 640 for text in a GIF, and twice
life size for art).
.TP
.BI \-F " FONT" "\fR, " \-\-font " FONT"
The font for text: a family, a file, or sans, serif, mono, impact, script ...
.TP
.B \-\-list\-fonts
List the fonts found and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-ink " COLOR" "\fR, " \-\-paper " COLOR"
The colour of the lines, and of the page.
.TP
.BR \-\-dark ", " \-\-light
Draw for a dark background (light lines on black), or a light one (dark lines on white).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text ", " \-\-art
The input is text to set in a font, even if it names a file or comes from a pipe; or terminal art, even if it looks like a few
words.  Without either, plain words are text and anything else is art.
.TP
.B \-\-3d\fR, \fB\-\-spin\fR, \fB\-\-sway\fR [\fIDEG\fR], \fB\-\-pop
3D and animation: a lit solid on a shadow; turning; swinging; or 2D to 3D, the picture starting flat on the screen and
popping out of it (add
.B \-\-spin
or
.B \-\-sway
to go on).
.TP
.BI \-\-material " NAME" "\fR, " \-\-depth " F\fR, " \-\-view " YAW,PITCH,ROLL\fR, " \-\-turns " N\fR, " \-\-fps " N"
The look and the motion: rainbow, chrome, gold, superhero, sunset, aqua, blues, purple, slate, lime, cornflower, graphite,
brick, cobalt, horizon, or #rrggbb; depth as a fraction of the picture's shorter side.
.TP
.B \-\-scroll\fR [\fIDIR\fR]\fB, \-\-window \fIN\fB, \-\-speed \fIN
Scrolling text, like the CP/M LARGE letter program: the picture slides through a window, round and round, until Ctrl\-C on a
terminal, or as a looping GIF.
.I DIR
is left (the default), right, up (rolling credits) or down.
.B \-\-window
is how much shows at once, in times the picture's height (its width going up or down; 5), and
.B \-\-speed
how fast, in that unit a second (3 sideways, 0.2 up or down).  A 3D look (\fB\-\-3d\fR, \fB\-P gold\fR) scrolls too.  Give the
words first or after the flag (text that scrolls sideways is set on one line: line breaks become spaces):
.B bidet \-\-scroll "Hello, World"
works.  For the rest (the gap, the start, the lap time) see the
.B scroll
operation in
.BR bifop (1).
.TP
.B \-\-time\-machine
Your text through banner (1983), FIGlet (1991), TOIlet (2004) and BIDeT (2020), then popping out of the screen: an animation.
.B \-\-stage\-time
sets the seconds for each era.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-show\-pipeline
Print the pipeline and do not run it.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Say what pipeline was run; print the version.
.TP
.BI \-\-bifin " FLAGS" "\fR, " \-\-bifop " OPS\fR, " \-\-bifout " FLAGS"
Extra flags for a stage, as one quoted string (a single flag: \fB\-\-bifout=\-\-erase=bg\fR).
.PP
Everything else is in
.BR "bidet \-\-help" .
.SH PRESETS
A preset is a section of
.IR presets.ini :
flags for the three tools, and for the 3D ones a
.B solid
(the look: material, depth, view ...), a
.B motion
(\fBpop+hold+spin\fR, \fBspin\fR, \fBsway\fR), a
.B scroll
(\fBleft window=5 start=enter\fR) and
.B uses
(other presets applied first).  There are shapes (arc, squeeze, wave, up ...), colours (matrix, amber, neon ...), tracers for
picture-style ASCII (sketch, trace, contour), 3D looks (chrome, rainbow, gold ...), motions (popout, turntable, swing, rise) and scrolls (ticker, cpm, credits).
Add your own in
.I ~/.config/bidet/presets.ini
(a section there replaces the preset of the same name).
.SH TERMINALS
On a terminal that reports SIXEL, a still is drawn there and an animation is played; one that does not gets the kitty or iTerm2
protocol if that is what it is, else coloured text, which plays an animation too.  SIXEL support differs from terminal
to terminal, and animation puts it under the most strain.  It has been tested thoroughly only in mintty, where it works well.
.B Your mileage may vary
(YMMV) in other terminals, including Windows Terminal, where animations show noise and leftover pixels of earlier frames.
Cygwin's own console (a Windows Terminal tab that runs bash directly) swallows the terminal's background-colour query, so a dark
page is assumed and a note is printed once; use mintty, run bash on a pty with wincygterm (CWPutils/toolbox), give
.B \-b
a colour, or log in from a PowerShell tab with Windows' ssh
.RB ( "C:\eWindows\eSystem32\eOpenSSH\essh.exe MACHINE" )
so the shell is a pty (this can be the command line of a Windows Terminal profile); animations are then clean.  See
.BR bifout (1).
The dependable way to see an animation is a GIF:
.BR "bidet \-P popout Hello \-o hello.gif" .
See
.BR bifout (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I presets.ini
The presets that come with bidet.
.TP
.I ~/.config/bidet/presets.ini
Your own; also a file named by
.BR $BIDET_PRESETS .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bifin (1),
.BR bifop (1),
.BR bifout (1).
The gallery of every preset is in README.md (and
.B python gallery.py
draws it).
